{"acceptance_length": 7.127659574468085, "acceptance_lengths": [3, 2, 2, 5, 4, 11, 5, 3, 8, 10, 3, 5, 9, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 9, 3, 2, 7, 5, 8, 1, 2, 6, 10, 3, 14, 8, 16, 14, 16, 4, 7, 9, 9, 12, 10, 3, 14, 16, 11, 16, 6, 10], "accepted_draft_lengths": [2, 1, 1, 4, 3, 10, 4, 2, 7, 9, 2, 4, 8, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 8, 2, 1, 6, 4, 7, 0, 1, 5, 9, 2, 13, 7, 15, 13, 15, 3, 6, 8, 8, 11, 9, 2, 13, 15, 10, 15, 5, 9], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 21115003904, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 21114998784, "benchmark": "gsm8k", "completion": "We are given the following relationships:\n\n1. **Samantha has 12 fewer paintings than Shelley.**\n2. **Shelley has 8 more paintings than Kim.**\n3. **Samantha has 27 paintings.**\n\nWe are asked to find **how many paintings Kim has**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the information about Samantha and Shelley\n\nWe are told:\n\n> Samantha has 12 fewer paintings than Shelley.\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\n- $ S $ = number of paintings Samantha has  \n- $ Sh $ = number of paintings Shelley has\n\nFrom the statement:\n\n$$\nS = Sh - 12\n$$\n\nWe are told that $ S = 27 $, so:\n\n$$\n27 = Sh - 12\n$$\n\nSolve for $ Sh $:\n\n$$\nSh = 27 + 12 = 39\n$$\n\nSo, **Shelley has 39 paintings**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the relationship between Shelley and Kim\n\nWe are told:\n\n> Shelley has 8 more paintings than Kim.\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\n- $ K $ = number of paintings Kim has\n\nFrom the statement:\n\n$$\nSh = K + 8\n$$\n\nWe already found that $ Sh = 39 $, so:\n\n$$\n39 = K + 8\n$$\n\nSolve for $ K $:\n\n$$\nK = 39 - 8 = 31\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{31}\n$$\n\nKim has **31 paintings**.", "decode_seconds": 1.714770636986941, "draft_calls": 47, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 64, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "a53b21494b39f7139d3a04b206a5698dbf22fbd0fa7192f8f9cc577bcf4a59e9", "method": "relay_base", "output_hash": "c80e3f4bb7f912b2e97cb1315140157d49b961539cf6975958112b9c5d36a15c", "output_tokens": 334, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 2